$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>Where wrong-forum casualties actually happen</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Civil suit vs commercial court</strong> — a money suit filed on the ordinary side is returned for presentation to the commercial division, or vice versa; the time before return is the classic Section 14 candidate.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Consumer complaint for a commercial supply</strong> — a supplier\'s complaint dismissed because the transaction was for a commercial purpose; the years before dismissal can be excluded in the later suit if diligence is shown.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Territorial and pecuniary misfires</strong> — plaints returned under Order VII Rule 10; on re-presentation the suit is treated as fresh, making Section 14 the bridge across the gap.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Arbitration route errors</strong> — Section 34 petitions filed in a court lacking jurisdiction, or claims pursued in court despite an arbitration clause before being referred; the exclusion doctrine has been applied to both, within statutory limits.</div>
</div>

<h2>The two load-bearing phrases</h2>
<p><strong>&ldquo;Due diligence and good faith&rdquo;</strong> does not demand infallibility — it demands honesty and care. A claimant who chose a forum on an arguable view of jurisdiction, prosecuted the matter actively, and moved promptly after the adverse order will ordinarily qualify. One who parked a claim in an obviously incompetent forum to buy time, or who slept for months after the return of the plaint, will not. The conduct is judged from the earlier proceeding\'s record: dates of filing, appearances, and the interval between the forum\'s refusal and the fresh institution.</p>
<p><strong>&ldquo;Defect of jurisdiction or other cause of a like nature&rdquo;</strong> extends beyond territorial and pecuniary incompetence to defects which prevent the forum from deciding the merits at all — but it does not cover dismissal on merits, on limitation, or for default. The touchstone: the earlier forum must have been <em>unable to entertain</em> the claim, not merely unwilling to allow it.</p>

<h2>Working the exclusion</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Plead it specifically</strong> — the fresh plaint or application should set out the earlier proceeding, its dates, the order by which it failed, and the computation showing the suit is in time after exclusion. Section 14 is not applied on silence.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Prove the record</strong> — file the earlier plaint or petition, the order of return or dismissal, and the certified order sheet establishing continuous prosecution.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Mind the arithmetic</strong> — the excluded period runs from institution to termination of the earlier proceeding, including time for obtaining certified copies; the days between termination and fresh filing are NOT excluded and must fit within what remains of limitation.</div>
</div>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>Preventive practice:</strong> when a jurisdictional objection surfaces in a pending recovery matter, do not fight it to the bitter end as a matter of pride. Evaluate early whether to seek return or withdrawal-with-liberty and re-file in the right forum while limitation (with exclusion) comfortably survives — the longer the wrong-forum battle runs, the more the eventual Section 14 argument depends on a court\'s generosity about diligence.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Section 14 is a provision courts construe liberally — the Supreme Court has repeatedly called it a tool for advancing justice rather than a trap of technicality. Liberal construction, however, attaches to the conditions once engaged, not to their proof. The claimant who documents an honest forum error survives it; the one who treats the section as an afterthought in final arguments usually does not.</p>
</div>
